1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,690
QYLD, the Nasdaq-100 covered call ETF
said to rarely eat into principal.

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:08,400
If you spend the payouts monthly,
is the principal really safe?

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:13,300
Let's backtest it against QQQ, an
ETF that tracks the Nasdaq-100.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:17,100
In December 2013, we buy $100,000 of each.

5
00:00:17,550 --> 00:00:22,000
From QYLD we take the payouts;
from QQQ we sell the same amount.

6
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:24,370
The fee is 0.25%.

7
00:00:25,370 --> 00:00:26,960
Now let's roll the dates.

8
00:00:27,410 --> 00:00:31,170
The orange line is the QYLD
account; the blue line, QQQ.

9
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:34,640
Yellow dotted line: the
principal, the $100,000 put in.

10
00:00:35,090 --> 00:00:38,130
The top-center number: the same
cash both accounts took out.

11
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:42,820
QQQ's small dividends are reinvested,
and taxes aren't included.

12
00:00:43,270 --> 00:00:47,070
The first payout, in
January 2014, was $1,024.

13
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:53,680
In March 2014, the QYLD account was
3.4% above principal; it seemed safe.

14
00:00:54,130 --> 00:00:58,420
But by February 2016, it
was 17.5% below principal.

15
00:00:58,870 --> 00:01:01,113
The next day, the QQQ
account briefly dipped

16
00:01:01,113 --> 00:01:03,050
below principal too; both were losing.

17
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:09,940
On March 15, 2018, the QYLD account
was 0.9% above principal, back on top.

18
00:01:10,390 --> 00:01:17,010
But by December 24, QYLD fell 21%
and QQQ 19.8%, about the same.

19
00:01:17,460 --> 00:01:21,830
In November 2019, the cash both
accounts took out passed $50,000.

20
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:29,080
On March 16, 2020, in the COVID crash, the
QYLD account was 28.3% below principal.

21
00:01:29,530 --> 00:01:34,090
The same day, the QQQ account
was 41.1% above principal.

22
00:01:35,090 --> 00:01:39,810
November 19, 2021: QQQ's
highest price up to that day.

23
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:47,500
That day, the QYLD account was 8.2% below
principal; QQQ's was 3.16 times principal.

24
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:50,100
White dotted lines: that day's values.

25
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:53,310
Then in 2022, a long decline began.

26
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:59,600
By October 14, the QYLD account
was down 33.2% from November 19.

27
00:02:00,050 --> 00:02:06,320
The QQQ account fell 37.8% by
December 28, a slightly bigger drop.

28
00:02:06,770 --> 00:02:11,550
Even during this fall, both accounts
took out the same cash: $8,720.

29
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,140
And the recovery?

30
00:02:13,590 --> 00:02:19,520
On February 7, 2024, after 810 days, the
QQQ account topped its November 19 value.

31
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:25,310
In March 2025, the cash taken out
passed the $100,000 principal.

32
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:30,880
In the April 8 plunge, the QYLD
account was 40.4% below principal.

33
00:02:31,330 --> 00:02:35,160
The same day, the QQQ account
was 2.98 times principal.

34
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:38,880
October 15, 2026: the results.

35
00:02:39,330 --> 00:02:43,410
Both accounts took out cash
148 times, about $110,000 each.

36
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:49,540
The QYLD account has about $75,000
left, 25.3% below principal.

37
00:02:49,990 --> 00:02:54,070
And it never got back above
its November 19, 2021 value.

38
00:02:54,520 --> 00:03:00,330
The QQQ account has about $510,000 left:
6.83 times as much, for the same cash out.

39
00:03:00,780 --> 00:03:06,210
The price of 1 QYLD share also
fell from $25.04 to $18.74.

40
00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:11,060
On 94.7% of all trading days, the
QYLD account was below principal.

41
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:14,340
Why such a big gap?

42
00:03:14,790 --> 00:03:17,600
Let's see how a covered
call makes its payouts.

43
00:03:18,050 --> 00:03:22,930
A covered call ETF holds stocks and sells
the right to buy them at a set price.

44
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:27,210
The money from selling that right
comes in monthly and funds the payouts.

45
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:30,072
But if the price rises
above the set price,

46
00:03:30,072 --> 00:03:32,820
the gain above it goes to
the buyer of the right.

47
00:03:33,270 --> 00:03:37,190
On the way down, it falls less only by
the money received, so nearly as much.

48
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:40,430
We checked whether that's
true using daily moves.

49
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:44,800
QQQ rose more than 1%
in a day on 595 days.

50
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:53,010
On those days QQQ rose 1.84% on average;
QYLD rose 0.95%, keeping just 51.3%.

51
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:57,460
QQQ fell more than 1%
in a day on 498 days.

52
00:03:57,910 --> 00:04:05,350
On those days QQQ fell 2.04% on average
and QYLD 1.28%, a full 62.7% of it.

53
00:04:06,350 --> 00:04:08,390
Was the start date just bad?

54
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:14,200
From January 2014 to October 2021,
we started a new account every month.

55
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:17,850
Each started on the month's first
trading day: 94 accounts in all.

56
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:21,020
Each account withdraws for 5
years under the same rules.

57
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:26,030
After 5 years, 83 of the 94 QYLD
accounts were below principal.

58
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:29,310
Not one QQQ account was below principal.

59
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:32,690
In all 94, the QQQ account had more left.

60
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:39,540
The median: QYLD 18.7% below
principal, QQQ 1.49 times principal.

61
00:04:39,990 --> 00:04:46,490
The worst was the QYLD account started
in January 2018: 35.9% below principal.

62
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:50,850
Shorten it to 3 years, and more
accounts get caught in the 2022 drop.

63
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:55,140
Then even 19 of 118 QQQ
accounts were below principal.

64
00:04:55,590 --> 00:04:58,750
And 81 QYLD accounts were below principal.

65
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:02,550
Still, in all 118, the
QQQ account had more left.

66
00:05:03,550 --> 00:05:05,830
Same for other covered call ETFs?

67
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:10,110
The Nasdaq-100, which QYLD
follows, tends to swing a lot.

68
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:15,650
So we also tested XYLD, a covered
call ETF on the calmer S&P 500.

69
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:19,860
It's compared with SPY, an
ETF that tracks the S&P 500.

70
00:05:20,310 --> 00:05:25,430
Over 13.3 years from June 2013,
about $110,000 was taken out.

71
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:29,318
The XYLD account grew 5.4% over principal,

72
00:05:29,318 --> 00:05:33,330
but the SPY account reached
3.33 times principal.

73
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:37,050
Across 100 accounts of 5 years
with different start months,

74
00:05:37,050 --> 00:05:38,380
SPY had more every time.

75
00:05:38,830 --> 00:05:42,100
And 60 of the 100 XYLD
accounts were below principal.

76
00:05:42,550 --> 00:05:45,840
JEPI is a covered call ETF
that works a bit differently.

77
00:05:46,290 --> 00:05:50,220
It mixes stocks with notes that
work like selling that right to buy.

78
00:05:50,670 --> 00:05:56,180
Over 6.4 years from May 2020,
about $61,000 was taken out.

79
00:05:56,630 --> 00:05:59,899
The JEPI account grew
14.2% over principal,

80
00:05:59,899 --> 00:06:03,320
but the SPY account was
1.79 times principal.

81
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:07,800
So far we left out taxes.
What if we add them?

82
00:06:08,250 --> 00:06:12,730
Many countries withhold 15% of
US ETF payouts when they're paid.

83
00:06:13,180 --> 00:06:18,620
With 15% withheld, the cash in hand
from QYLD drops to about $96,000.

84
00:06:19,070 --> 00:06:23,330
The QQQ account sells only that
much too, leaving about $560,000.

85
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:28,180
The QYLD account has the same
shares, so it's still about $75,000.

86
00:06:28,630 --> 00:06:32,240
Some cover living costs elsewhere
and reinvest the payouts.

87
00:06:32,690 --> 00:06:38,210
Doing that, QYLD came to about
$290,000 and QQQ about $950,000.

88
00:06:38,660 --> 00:06:42,090
Even reinvesting, QQQ
had 3.25 times as much.

89
00:06:43,090 --> 00:06:44,210
To sum up.

90
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:47,956
The QYLD account spending
its payouts monthly

91
00:06:47,956 --> 00:06:50,740
lost 25.3% of principal in 12.8 years.

92
00:06:51,190 --> 00:06:55,750
The QQQ account, selling to take out the
same cash, had 5.1 times principal left.

93
00:06:56,200 --> 00:07:00,240
Even with other start months,
QQQ always had more left.

94
00:07:00,690 --> 00:07:03,366
XYLD and JEPI kept their principal,

95
00:07:03,366 --> 00:07:06,960
but had less than SPY
taking out the same cash.

96
00:07:07,410 --> 00:07:11,490
Smaller gains on the way up, most of the
losses on the way down: that gap added up.

97
00:07:12,490 --> 00:07:16,150
In this period, the Nasdaq-100
and S&P 500 rose sharply.

98
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:20,360
In a period when prices stayed flat
for long, results could differ.

99
00:07:20,810 --> 00:07:24,490
Payouts were counted as taken on the
ex-dividend date, not the pay date.

100
00:07:24,940 --> 00:07:30,300
Amounts taken out match QYLD's payouts,
so they may differ from a real budget.

101
00:07:30,750 --> 00:07:33,016
Taxes by account type, health premiums,

102
00:07:33,016 --> 00:07:35,630
inflation and exchange
rates aren't included.

103
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:41,600
And the QQQ side had to sit
through drops like 37.8% in 2022.

104
00:07:42,050 --> 00:07:45,340
This isn't investment advice; it's the
result of checking rules with code.

105
00:07:46,340 --> 00:07:50,110
If you have a hypothesis to backtest
next, tell us in the comments.

106
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:52,110
Thanks for watching.
